@anon.a2b023d1a4e2834952d16152dce23780.988 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [4902 x i8] }> <{ [4902 x i8] c"A color in a specific color space.\0A\0ATypst supports:\0A- sRGB through the [`rgb` function]($color.rgb)\0A- Device CMYK through [`cmyk` function]($color.cmyk)\0A- D65 Gray through the [`luma` function]($color.luma)\0A- Oklab through the [`oklab` function]($color.oklab)\0A- Oklch through the [`oklch` function]($color.oklch)\0A- Linear RGB through the [`color.linear-rgb` function]($color.linear-rgb)\0A- HSL through the [`color.hsl` function]($color.hsl)\0A- HSV through the [`color.hsv` function]($color.hsv)\0A\0A\0A# Example\0A\0A```example\0A#rect(fill: aqua)\0A```\0A\0A# Predefined colors\0ATypst defines the following built-in colors:\0A\0A| Color     | Definition         |\0A|-----------|:-------------------|\0A| `black`   | `{luma(0)}`        |\0A| `gray`    | `{luma(170)}`      |\0A| `silver`  | `{luma(221)}`      |\0A| `white`   | `{luma(255)}`      |\0A| `navy`    | `{rgb(\22#001f3f\22)}` |\0A| `blue`    | `{rgb(\22#0074d9\22)}` |\0A| `aqua`    | `{rgb(\22#7fdbff\22)}` |\0A| `teal`    | `{rgb(\22#39cccc\22)}` |\0A| `eastern` | `{rgb(\22#239dad\22)}` |\0A| `purple`  | `{rgb(\22#b10dc9\22)}` |\0A| `fuchsia` | `{rgb(\22#f012be\22)}` |\0A| `maroon`  | `{rgb(\22#85144b\22)}` |\0A| `red`     | `{rgb(\22#ff4136\22)}` |\0A| `orange`  | `{rgb(\22#ff851b\22)}` |\0A| `yellow`  | `{rgb(\22#ffdc00\22)}` |\0A| `olive`   | `{rgb(\22#3d9970\22)}` |\0A| `green`   | `{rgb(\22#2ecc40\22)}` |\0A| `lime`    | `{rgb(\22#01ff70\22)}` |\0A\0AThe predefined colors and the most important color constructors are\0Aavailable globally and also in the color type's scope, so you can write\0Aeither `color.red` or just `red`.\0A\0A```preview\0A#let colors = (\0A  \22black\22, \22gray\22, \22silver\22, \22white\22,\0A  \22navy\22, \22blue\22, \22aqua\22, \22teal\22,\0A  \22eastern\22, \22purple\22, \22fuchsia\22,\0A  \22maroon\22, \22red\22, \22orange\22, \22yellow\22,\0A  \22olive\22, \22green\22, \22lime\22,\0A)\0A\0A#set text(font: \22PT Sans\22)\0A#set page(width: auto)\0A#grid(\0A  columns: 9,\0A  gutter: 10pt,\0A  ..colors.map(name => {\0A      let col = eval(name)\0A      let luminance = luma(col).components().first()\0A      set text(fill: white) if luminance < 50%\0A      set square(stroke: black) if col == white\0A      set align(center + horizon)\0A      square(size: 50pt,  fill: col, name)\0A  })\0A)\0A```\0A\0A# Predefined color maps\0ATypst also includes a number of preset color maps that can be used for\0A[gradients]($gradient.linear). These are simply arrays of colors defined in\0Athe module `color.map`.\0A\0A```example\0A#circle(fill: gradient.linear(..color.map.crest))\0A```\0A\0A| Map        | Details                                                     |\0A|------------|:------------------------------------------------------------|\0A| `turbo`    | A perceptually uniform rainbow-like color map. Read [this blog post](https://ai.googleblog.com/2019/08/turbo-improved-rainbow-colormap-for.html) for more details. |\0A| `cividis`  | A blue to gray to yellow color map. See [this blog post](https://bids.github.io/colormap/) for more details. |\0A| `rainbow`  | Cycles through the full color spectrum. This color map is best used by setting the interpolation color space to [HSL]($color.hsl). The rainbow gradient is **not suitable** for data visualization because it is not perceptually uniform, so the differences between values become unclear to your readers. It should only be used for decorative purposes. |\0A| `spectral` | Red to yellow to blue color map.                            |\0A| `viridis`  | A purple to teal to yellow color map.                       |\0A| `inferno`  | A black to red to yellow color map.                         |\0A| `magma`    | A black to purple to yellow color map.                      |\0A| `plasma`   | A purple to pink to yellow color map.                       |\0A| `rocket`   | A black to red to white color map.                          |\0A| `mako`     | A black to teal to yellow color map.                        |\0A| `vlag`     | A light blue to white to red color map.                     |\0A| `icefire`  | A light teal to black to yellow color map.                  |\0A| `flare`    | A orange to purple color map that is perceptually uniform.  |\0A| `crest`    | A blue to white to red color map.                           |\0A\0ASome popular presets are not included because they are not available under a\0Afree licence. Others, like\0A[Jet](https://jakevdp.github.io/blog/2014/10/16/how-bad-is-your-colormap/),\0Aare not included because they are not color blind friendly. Feel free to use\0Aor create a package with other presets that are useful to you!\0A\0A```preview\0A#set page(width: auto, height: auto)\0A#set text(font: \22PT Sans\22, size: 8pt)\0A\0A#let maps = (\0A  \22turbo\22, \22cividis\22, \22rainbow\22, \22spectral\22,\0A  \22viridis\22, \22inferno\22, \22magma\22, \22plasma\22,\0A  \22rocket\22, \22mako\22, \22vlag\22, \22icefire\22,\0A  \22flare\22, \22crest\22,\0A)\0A\0A#stack(dir: ltr, spacing: 3pt, ..maps.map((name) => {\0A  let map = eval(\22color.map.\22 + name)\0A  stack(\0A    dir: ttb,\0A    block(\0A      width: 15pt,\0A      height: 100pt,\0A      fill: gradient.linear(..map, angle: 90deg),\0A    ),\0A    block(\0A      width: 15pt,\0A      height: 32pt,\0A      move(dy: 8pt, rotate(90deg, name)),\0A    ),\0A  )\0A}))\0A```" }>, align 1
